Before: `ReportGenerator` reaches directly into `Database`'s internal connection object.

```java
class ReportGenerator {
    void generate(Database db) {
        Connection conn = db.rawConnection;
        conn.executeQuery("SELECT * FROM sales");
    }
}
```

Any change to how `Database` manages connections breaks `ReportGenerator` directly. After: an interface hides the internal detail.

```java
interface SalesReader {
    List<Sale> readSales();
}
class ReportGenerator {
    void generate(SalesReader reader) {
        List<Sale> sales = reader.readSales();
    }
}
```

`ReportGenerator` now only knows about `readSales()` — `Database` can change its connection handling, pooling, or even its underlying engine, and `ReportGenerator` never notices.
